# To attribute; to assign; to adjudge.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1777|author={{w|Richard Brinsley Sheridan}}|title={{w|The School for Scandal}}|page=6|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FzZBAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA6|passage=I don't wonder at people '''giving''' him to me for a lover.}}
# To communicate or announce (advice, tidings, etc.); to pronounce or utter (an opinion, a judgment, a shout, etc.).
#: {{ux|en|The umpire finally '''gave''' his decision: the ball was out.}}
# {{lb|en|dated|_|or|_|religion}} To grant power, permission, destiny, etc. (especially to a person); to allot; to allow.
#* {{RQ:KJV|1=Deuteronomy|2=12|3=1|text=These ''are'' the statutes and judgments, which ye shall observe to do in the land, which the Lᴏʀᴅ God of thy fathers '''giveth''' thee to possess it, all the days that ye live upon the earth.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1700|author=[[w:Nicholas Rowe (writer)|Nicholas Rowe]]|title={{w|The Ambitious Stepmother}}|page=13|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DMzkD1EWOR8C&pg=PA13|passage=once again<br>'Tis '''given''' me to behold my friend.}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=III|passage=Then '''give''' thy friend to shed the sacred wine.}}
# {{lb|en|reflexive}} To devote or apply (oneself).
#: {{ux|en|The soldiers '''give''' themselves to plunder.}}
#: {{ux|en|That boy is '''given''' to fits of bad temper.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To become soft or moist.
#* {{quote-book|en|1590|John Smyth|A Discourse {{...}}concerning{{...}}weapons|passage=Some moyst weather hath‥caused the powder to '''give''' and danke.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To shed tears; to weep.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|IV|iii|page=94|passage=Whose eyes do never '''give''' / But through lust and laughter.}}
